        <h1>Exercise Name: High-to-Low Cable Fly (High Cable Crossover)</h1>
        <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>
This 3D anatomical visualization demonstrates High-to-Low Cable Chest Flyes (High Cable Crossovers). The video highlights how placing the pulleys above shoulder height creates a downward cable angle that directly aligns with the muscle fibers of the lower chest (Pectoralis Major — Abdominal Head), maximizing target contraction.

 Exercise Name: High-to-Low Cable Fly (High Cable Crossover)
 Target Muscles: Lower Chest (Pectoralis Major — Abdominal/Sternal Head)
 Secondary Muscles: Anterior Deltoids (front shoulders), Core (for body stabilization)

</div></div>
<pre>
<h1>How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1>

 <u><b><h4>1. Setup</h4></b></u>: Set the pulleys on a dual cable crossover station to the highest position. Attach single D-handles to both sides. Grab a handle in each hand, step forward to lift the weights off the stack, and use a staggered stance for stability.
 <u><b><h4>2. Stance & Form</h4></b></u>: Lean slightly forward at the hips while keeping your back straight and chest lifted. Maintain a soft, fixed bend in your elbows throughout the movement.
 <u><b><h4>3. Fly Phase (Concentric)</h4></b></u>: Exhale as you bring your hands downward and inward in a sweeping arc, guiding the handles toward the lower stomach or waist level until your hands meet in front of your body.
 <u><b><h4>4. Peak Squeeze</h4></b></u>: Pause for a split second at the bottom position, squeezing your lower chest muscles tightly together.
 <u><b><h4>5. Return Phase (Eccentric)</h4></b></u>: Inhale as you slowly allow the cables to pull your hands back up to the starting position under full control until you feel a complete stretch across your pecs.


<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Lower Chest Isolation:</b> Pulling downward against elevated cable resistance directly isolates the lower pectoral fibers to build defined lower chest separation.
 <b>◉Constant Cable Tension:</b> Unlike decline bench dumbbell presses, cables keep tension on the muscles even at the peak contraction point.
 <b>◉Reduced Shoulder Strain:</b> Allows for a smooth, natural joint movement path that reduces unnecessary pressure on the rotator cuff.



<h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Muscle Building (Hypertrophy) | 3 – 4 | 10 – 12 | 60 – 90 seconds |
| Endurance / Definition | 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 | 45 – 60 seconds |
| Strength | 3 – 4 | 8 – 10 | 90 seconds |

Keep your core braced and avoid using momentum to swing your body forward and backward while pulling the cables.
